WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:08.280
If the cortex kept the bulb's map, where would one glomerulus send its output?

2
00:00:09.283 --> 00:00:11.683
A topographic map keeps neighbours together.

3
00:00:12.603 --> 00:00:15.543
Then one glomerulus should land in one patch of cortex.

4
00:00:16.667 --> 00:00:20.007
Sosulski and colleagues asked whether the wiring does that.

5
00:00:20.747 --> 00:00:24.347
They put a tracer into a single glomerulus of an adult mouse.

6
00:00:25.367 --> 00:00:28.887
Five days later, they imaged where the labelled axons went.

7
00:00:29.867 --> 00:00:34.567
They looked in two targets: piriform cortex and, for comparison, the cortical

8
00:00:34.567 --> 00:00:35.567
amygdala.

9
00:00:36.067 --> 00:00:37.267
Pause and predict.

10
00:00:38.267 --> 00:00:42.227
In piriform cortex, will the axons gather in a patch or spread out?

11
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:44.307
Must both targets agree?

12
00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:59.600
In piriform cortex the axons spread diffusely, with no apparent spatial preference.

13
00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:04.873
In the cortical amygdala, the same glomerulus gave stereotyped patches.

14
00:01:05.933 --> 00:01:10.693
One tracer in one glomerulus showed two different rules in two different targets.

15
00:01:11.733 --> 00:01:15.753
It would have been easy to conclude that the bulb projects at random everywhere.

16
00:01:16.593 --> 00:01:18.653
The cortical amygdala shows it does not.

17
00:01:19.667 --> 00:01:22.227
So the scatter is not a property of the whole bulb.

18
00:01:23.047 --> 00:01:26.027
It was seen in piriform cortex, not in the amygdala.

19
00:01:27.150 --> 00:01:29.530
A tracer image shows where axons go.

20
00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:32.790
It does not show which synapses each neuron makes.

21
00:01:33.800 --> 00:01:36.420
Recordings of responses tell the same story.

22
00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:41.980
Stettler and Axel imaged odour responses in piriform cortex of living mice.

23
00:01:43.033 --> 00:01:45.533
An odour activated a dispersed ensemble.

24
00:01:46.493 --> 00:01:49.933
Its neurons were scattered across the area, not gathered in a patch.

25
00:01:50.967 --> 00:01:54.807
A neuron's receptive field is the set of odours that excite it.

26
00:01:55.667 --> 00:02:00.167
A single piriform neuron could respond to chemically dissimilar odours.

27
00:02:01.117 --> 00:02:04.597
Its receptive field was discontinuous in chemical space.

28
00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:09.227
This does not say piriform carries no chemical information.

29
00:02:10.207 --> 00:02:12.907
It says the arrangement is not a topographic map.

30
00:02:13.867 --> 00:02:16.187
Diffuse is also not the same as random.

31
00:02:17.267 --> 00:02:21.947
Later projection mapping found spatial structure in how bulb neurons route their

32
00:02:21.947 --> 00:02:25.107
axons. It was not a map of odour chemistry.

33
00:02:26.167 --> 00:02:29.727
So the scatter is real, and it belongs to piriform cortex.

34
00:02:30.807 --> 00:02:33.907
Next comes the harder question: how can a scatter keep chemistry?
